The '''''First Mahik War''''' was fought from 11 August 1538 to 7 April 1542 between an alliance of the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina state]] and the [[Zong colonial administration]] on one side and the chiefdoms of the region north of the Hahkina on the other. It resulted in the partition of that region between the two allies and in the destruction of most of its population.

The war takes its name from the Hahkina term [[mahik]], meaning "kingless", by which the Hahkina described the peoples to their north and west. The term was a pejorative, describing those peoples by the absence of a ruler of the centralised kind the Hahkina recognised. It was a category of peoples rather than the name of a nation, and its use as the name of the conflict is a later convention: contemporary Zong records do not use it.

The belligerents on that side are designated in modern accounts as the Kawesa chiefdoms. Kawesa is the collective name later applied to the peoples of the region, drawn from the name of one of the chiefdoms. It is not a name they used of themselves, and neither the Hahkina nor the Zong of the period used it.

The war was the first campaign undertaken by the Zong colonies in the New World under the doctrine of coastal holdings, and the only one conducted as an offensive alliance with an indigenous state. It is generally treated as the opening conflict of the [[First Lurandian Native Wars]].

== Background ==

=== Legal ground of the war ===

The war was prosecuted on a claim of jurisdiction rather than as a campaign against foreign powers. In the negotiations for the [[Treaty of Anping]] the colonial administration advanced the position that the northern chiefdoms were not independent polities but subjects in rebellion, and the claim also fixed the boundary between the territory the Zong were to hold and that left to the Hahkina.

The claim rested on the posthumous title conferred on [[Hokta]], chief of the Akwesa, who had died in 1527. Hokta was declared to have been King of the Northern Mahiks — the ruler of the whole region rather than of the Akwesa alone. On that basis the administration argued that the northern chiefdoms had all been subject to him; that Hokta had granted his land to the [[Sūn-heng Hâng]] and the company had conveyed it to the colony in 1535; and that the lands of the chiefdoms were therefore the colony's already. Chiefdoms that did not submit were consequently described in the Zong papers as rebellious rather than as belligerent.

The title is not attested in any document of Hokta's lifetime, and no chiefdom of the north is recorded as having acknowledged his authority. The claim was devised by [[Tân Bûn-khéng]] (陳文慶), the administration's negotiator, and is treated in the later literature as a legal construction devised to supply a ground of jurisdiction for the acquisition of the coast. No objection to it is recorded from any quarter.

The Hahkina did not dispute the claim. Their object was the land on their northern frontier, which the arrangement gave them, and the question of who had ruled the northern chiefdoms was not one in which they had an interest. Nothing in the Hahkina record indicates that the title was communicated to them or that Hokta was known to them, and the later literature treats their acceptance as a consequence of the territory they received rather than of any assessment of the Zong's title.

=== Peoples of the region ===

Historians have noted a difficulty in the naming of the peoples concerned. The collective term Kawesa, in current use, descends from Akwesa, the single chiefdom whose name and history were preserved — a circumstance attributable both to that chiefdom's commercial relationship with the colony and to the destruction of the remaining chiefdoms. The term thus designates a population by the name of one of its constituent groups.

The territory north of the Hahkina contained approximately seven chiefdoms speaking related but distinct languages. They had no common government, no central authority, and no established practice of joint diplomacy; each conducted its own relations with its neighbours and its own wars.

The Hahkina applied to these peoples the term [[mahik|''mahik'']], "kingless", which they used also of the Chidoha and Salowi peoples to the west. The term was pejorative, and was applied to peoples who lacked a ruler of the kind the Hahkina recognised. Zong colonial records of the period do not distinguish between the chiefdoms and treat them as a single population. Modern scholarship has retained this treatment, applying to them collectively the name [[Kawesa]], coined from the name of the single chiefdom Akwesa.

=== Zong interest ===

By the 1530s the Zong colonial administration had concluded that permanent territorial rule of the Lurandian interior was not achievable, and had adopted a policy of securing the Zong presence through a small number of defensible coastal settlements. Under this doctrine each settlement was to be a bounded holding — termed a territorial island in the colonial correspondence — surrounded by territory left to indigenous government.

The site at [[Tamsui]], selected during the coastal surveys of the preceding decades, lay on the coast of the northern lands and was occupied by one of the mahik chiefdoms.

=== Hahkina interest ===

The Hahkina were an agrarian state organised around a king and a regnal council, and the largest and most centralised indigenous polity the Zong had encountered in the region. They had contended with the southern mahik chiefdoms for generations, both for land and for access to the gold-bearing hills around the long lake to the north.

The Hahkina were unable to subdue the chiefdoms unaided, the chiefdoms being in aggregate more numerous than the Hahkina forces; but they could not combine against the Hahkina either, being separate polities with separate interests.

== Prelude ==

{{Main|Treaty of Anping}}

The alliance was concluded at [[Anping]] in 1538. An embassy from the Hahkina court, led by Nachiha, son of King Tsulhachi, travelled to the colonial capital — the first member of the Hahkina royal house recorded as having left Lurandia — and was received by Governor-General Ông Bûn-hêng.

The [[Treaty of Anping]] recognised the Hahkina as a sovereign state, fixed the Zong presence at defined coastal positions, and set the southern limit of Hahkina territory at the [[Tsuwasha]], the river separating the Hahkina from the mahik peoples of the southern country. It also provided for the campaign: the partition of the northern lands was a subject of the negotiation, and terms for the joint conquest were agreed in advance. The treaty was ratified by the government of [[Zong]] some months after its conclusion, before the opening of hostilities.

== Course of the war ==

The war opened on 11 August 1538, four months after the conclusion of the treaty. It was fought on two fronts: Zong forces operating against the coastal chiefdoms to secure the Tamsui harbour, and Hahkina forces advancing north from their frontier. The two allies pursued separate objectives and were not required to combine in a single operation.

The chiefdoms resisted as separate forces, and no authority existed capable of directing a general mobilisation or of concluding a surrender on behalf of the whole. The campaigns lasted three years and eight months.

The war ended on 7 April 1542 with the defeat of the chiefdoms and the partition of their territory between the two allies.

The north, including Tamsui and the coast, passed to the Zong and was subsequently constituted as part of the [[Sinchew]] colony. The south, adjoining the Hahkina frontier, passed to the Hahkina.

== Casualties ==

