The allied losses were borne overwhelmingly by the Hahkina. Zong committed approximately 4,000 colonial troops and sustained 430 killed and 1,430 wounded; the Hahkina committed some 45,000 men and sustained 11,340 killed and 18,432 wounded. The disparity reflects both the relative size of the forces and the nature of the campaign, in which Zong operations were confined chiefly to the coastal positions while Hahkina forces advanced into the interior.

No record of the deaths among the chiefdoms was kept, and no census existed against which losses could be measured. The estimates available in the later literature indicate that between eighty and ninety per cent of the population of the region was killed or enslaved. Survivors fled north and west into the territories of the [[Timara]] and the [[Salowi]].

The scale of the destruction followed from the objectives of the two allies. The Zong sought a harbour rather than subjects, and had adopted a policy of coastal holdings rather than territorial rule; the Hahkina sought land and the gold-bearing approaches. No administration was established over the chiefdoms, and the region was not governed as a conquered territory, its surviving population being dispersed rather than subjected.

== Territorial change ==

[[File:First Mahik War before and after.png|thumb|400px|Territorial arrangements at the Treaty of Anping, 1538 (left), and after the partition of 1542 (right).]]
<!-- Image prompt: two-panel historical map of western Lurandia; left panel shows the Hahkina territory in gold, the Kawesa/mahik lands in green, and Tamsui as a small red coastal enclave; right panel shows only two territories — the Sinchew colony in red and the Hahkina in gold; surrounding land neutral grey, no modern borders, no labels -->

At the conclusion of the Treaty of Anping the region consisted of two recognised territories and one unpartitioned one: the Hahkina state to the south, the Zong settlement at Tamsui on the coast, and the lands of the Kawesa chiefdoms to the north. After the war the third of these had ceased to exist as an independent territory, its lands divided between the two allies.

== Aftermath ==

=== Akwesa ===

One people, the Akwesa, survived the war intact. It had occupied the site of the future Tamsui and had conducted direct dealings with the colonial administration before the war. Its bay had passed to the colony by purchase in 1535, and the settlement had been built on land it had granted in 1511. By 1542 it was a client of the colony rather than a target of it, and it continued thereafter as a minority community within the Sinchew colony.

Akwesa is the only chiefdom whose name is preserved in the colonial record, and the modern collective name for the peoples of the region descends from it.

=== Colonial split of 1544 ===

The acquisition of the northern coast removed the dependence of the Tamsui settlement on [[Louyang]]. Two years after the conclusion of the war the colonial territory was divided along the [[Qingjiang]] river into the [[Sinchew]] and [[Louyang]] colonies. The [[Tamsui Plot]] of 1543–1544 provided the occasion for the division; the war had removed the reason for retaining a single colonial administration.

=== Hahkina ===

The Hahkina obtained the southern lands and the gold-bearing approaches for which they had contended, and acquired in the Zong a neighbouring power with a land frontier rather than a distant one with a harbour. Relations between the two states continued after the war, but the alliance was not renewed on the same terms.

=== Later Mahik wars ===

The First Mahik War was the first of several campaigns conducted by the Zong colonies and their indigenous allies against the mahik peoples. A later conflict, sometimes termed the Second Mahik War, was fought against the Salowi and Chidoha peoples of the west.

The war is treated in modern Sinchew as a historical injustice and is included in Sinchewese school curricula as an instance of the conduct of the colonial period. The modern account does not distinguish between the Zong colonial administration and the Sinchewese state that inherited its territory, and presents the events as part of the national inheritance rather than as the actions of a separate power.

